{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nFour guards in `tests/unit/test_subcall_class_selection.py` each call\n`_run_request_sites()` (line 467), which re-derives the SAME immutable analysis of\nthe whole `src/rebar` tree: `test_the_provenance_analysis_can_see_the_sites_it_judges`\n(line 487), `test_no_run_request_inherits_the_raw_config_model` (line 495),\n`test_every_unfollowable_site_is_registered_with_a_reason` (line 515) and\n`test_neither_registry_has_stale_entries` (line 524). CI recorded 30.10s, 32.27s,\n31.02s and 32.50s for them in one contended run.\n\nThe analysis is expensive twice over, and the second reason is not in the ticket\ntitle. `_run_request_sites` walks `_SRC.rglob(\"*.py\")` and parses every file that\nmentions `RunRequest(`. For each site it calls `_verdict`, whose parameter-provenance\nbranch (lines 448-450) re-walks `sorted(_SRC.rglob(\"*.py\"))` and calls\n`ast.parse(caller_path.read_text())` on EVERY file in the tree, once per site it has\nto follow — which is why a single scan was profiled at ~8,033 parses over a tree\nwith far fewer files. So the repetition is both across the four tests and inside\neach one.\n\nThe analysis is a pure function of the committed source: no test mutates\n`src/rebar`, and the module's only autouse fixture (`class_table`, line 60) patches\n`llm_config._read_llm_file_table`, which the AST scan never consults.\n\n## Approach\n\nCompute the analysis once per test process, and stop the inner scan re-reading the\ntree.\n\n* Add a session-scoped fixture `run_request_sites` that calls `_run_request_sites()`\n  once and returns its list; the four guards take it as a parameter instead of\n  calling the function. A pytest fixture is bounded by the session that created it,\n  so the cached value cannot outlive the run or be served to a later session — the\n  staleness requirement is satisfied by construction rather than by an\n  invalidation rule that could itself be wrong. `_run_request_sites` stays a plain\n  function so the fixture, and any future caller, can still invoke it directly.\n* Memoise the per-path parse behind a module-level\n  `_parsed(path) -> ast.Module` keyed on the path, used by `_run_request_sites`,\n  `_functions` (line 355) and `_verdict`'s caller scan (line 450). Within one\n  process each file is then read and parsed at most once, which removes the inner\n  repetition the four tests were each paying in full.\n\nBoth halves are caching only: the same `(key, expr, verdict)` tuples are produced,\nin the same order, and every assertion, offender message and registry check is\nuntouched. Deriving the analysis once is the smallest change that removes the\nrepetition without weakening what the guards inspect — the alternative of narrowing\nthe scan (fewer files, cheaper heuristics) would trade coverage for time, and these\nguards exist precisely because a vacuous scan is the failure mode\n(`test_the_provenance_analysis_can_see_the_sites_it_judges` guards the guard).\n\n## Scope\n\n* `tests/unit/test_subcall_class_selection.py` — add `_parsed`, route the three\n  parse sites through it, add the session-scoped `run_request_sites` fixture, and\n  change the four guards to take it.\n\nNo production change: `src/rebar` is READ by the scan and not modified.\n\n## Testing\n\n* Happy path — all four guards still pass and still see the real tree:\n  `test_the_provenance_analysis_can_see_the_sites_it_judges` continues to assert\n  `>= 15` sites with a non-empty `config=` on each, which is what makes the other\n  three non-vacuous.\n* Equivalence check — the analysis produced through the fixture and the parse memo\n  is compared element-by-element against the result of the pre-change code on the\n  same tree, and must be identical.\n* Failure/edge path — a defect is seeded by adding a `RunRequest(config=cfg, ...)`\n  site to a real `src/rebar` module and confirming\n  `test_no_run_request_inherits_the_raw_config_model` FAILS naming that site\n  through the cached path. Seed reverted, not committed.\n* Whole-suite regression — the CI pytest command is green.\n\nNo wall-clock or timing-budget assertion is added; savings go in ticket comments.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `_run_request_sites` is invoked exactly once per test process when the four\n      guards run, verified by counting invocations with a temporary wrapper during\n      a focused run and reporting the count in a ticket comment.\n- [ ] The four guards' assertions, offender messages and both registry checks are\n      unchanged in meaning — verified by `git diff` showing no edit to any assert\n      body or message, only the parameter each test receives.\n- [ ] The list of `(key, expr, verdict)` tuples is byte-identical before and after\n      the change, verified by capturing it on `origin/main` and on the change and\n      comparing.\n- [ ] A seeded raw-config `RunRequest` site is still caught by\n      `test_no_run_request_inherits_the_raw_config_model`, with the offender key in\n      the failure message — evidence in a ticket comment, seed reverted.\n- [ ] The cache is a pytest session fixture plus a process-local parse memo, with\n      no on-disk artifact, so nothing survives the pytest session — verified by\n      `git diff` showing no file is written.\n- [ ] Before/after durations for the four guards are measured with\n      `pytest --durations` and posted as a ticket comment."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786604774048589001,"uuid":"f30315b3-06c1-4f29-9104-fcf8ecc38e11"}